ten/cover half\n4. Borrow availability: if SENEB becomes hard to borrow (fee >15%), cover \u2014 likely signals a corporate action\n5. Price action: if the stock breaks above $210 with a 10-day average volume >20k shares (possible short squeeze), cover immediately.\n\n**Post-mortem triggers for the short (what I will write if I am wrong):** Seneca's GM sustains >14%; the Green Giant frozen acquisition adds $250M+ revenue with 20%+ operating margin (i.e., B&G was failing to exploit it, and Seneca's scale turns it around); the market shifts from GAAP to FIFO EPS permanently, and the 82% insider ownership means the float is just not available for the short to ever be correct \u2014 the price is set on the margin by buyers who believe the upside story.\n\n**Alternate view on SENEA (Class A)**: Given the 10.8% price discount vs SENEB for identical economic rights, a long SENEA/short SENEB pair is NOT proposed \u2014 the share classes have different voting rights and liquidity, but a buyer at SENEA ($176) has a better entry than a buyer of SENEB ($195) for the same equity. However, that long thesis still fails my fair-value test and is not recommended.\n\n**Final judgment**: SENEB at $195.39 prices in a quality-compounder transition (15% margin, 3% growth, 13x multiple) that a 12-year recorded history of 3.7% median operating margins does not support. The stock has risen ~90% in 12 months to the top of its 52-week range, driven by record FIFO EPS headlines, while GAAP normalized earnings power supports $108. Probability-weighted intrinsic value: **$108.30** (50% CI $85-135; 80% CI $68-167). Enter a **short at market or on strength**, size **2%**, monitor monthly. **Re-evaluate the entire thesis immediately if gross margin (FIFO) confirms >14.5% for two consecutive quarters.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u25